Personal Expense Tracker – Detailed Report
1. Project Overview
The Personal Expense Tracker project focuses on analyzing personal financial spending data to understand how individuals distribute their monthly income across different expense categories. The purpose of this project is to identify major spending areas, observe monthly spending trends, and extract meaningful insights that can help in better budgeting and financial planning.
This analysis was performed using Python for data processing and visualization.
2. Dataset Description
The dataset used for this project contains monthly financial information for multiple users. Each record represents one user’s expense details for a specific month in a specific year.
The dataset includes the following major information:
UserID: Unique identifier for each user
Year: Year of record
Month: Month of record
Income: Total income earned by the user during the month
Expense categories such as:
Food
Groceries
Transport
Entertainment
Shopping
Rent
Bills
Healthcare
Education
Total_Exp: Total expense for the month
Savings: Remaining amount after expenses
Some additional calculated fields such as ratios for categories
3. Objectives of the Analysis
The main objectives of this project are:
To load and validate the dataset properly
To clean and prepare the dataset for analysis
To calculate meaningful spending metrics
To visualize spending patterns using charts
To generate insights that summarize user spending behavior
4. Data Cleaning and Preparation
Before performing analysis, the dataset was checked for data quality issues.
Cleaning steps performed:
The dataset was loaded and reviewed to confirm that all required columns were present.
Missing values were checked across all columns.
Data types were verified for numeric fields such as income and expenses.
Expense categories were selected and grouped to calculate totals and trends.
A combined time column (Year-Month) was created to support monthly trend analysis.
This preparation ensured the dataset was consistent and ready for accurate calculations.
5. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
Exploratory analysis was performed to understand the general structure of the dataset.
Key observations from EDA:
The dataset contains multiple users, each having monthly spending records.
Expenses are divided into different categories which helps in identifying spending priorities.
The dataset also provides total expenses and savings which makes it useful for budgeting insights.
6. Analysis Performed
6.1 Category-wise Spending Analysis
To understand where most of the money is spent, the total spending for each category was calculated by summing all values across users and months.
This analysis helps answer:
Which expense category has the highest spending?
Which categories contribute the most to overall monthly expenses?
6.2 Monthly Expense Trend Analysis
To understand how spending changes over time, monthly total expenses were calculated.
This analysis helps answer:
Which month has the highest spending?
Are expenses consistent or do they fluctuate?
Is there an increasing or decreasing spending trend?
6.3 Savings and Expense Relationship
Since the dataset includes savings, an important analysis was to observe the relationship between:
Income
Total expenses
Savings
This helps understand how much money users generally save after spending.
7. Visualizations Created
To make the analysis more understandable and professional, multiple visualizations were created.
Visualization 1: Total Spending by Category (Bar Chart)
A bar chart was created to show the total amount spent in each expense category.
This chart makes it easy to identify the categories that consume the most money.
Visualization 2: Monthly Total Expense Trend (Line Chart)
A line chart was created to represent how total expenses change across different months.
This chart helps observe patterns such as spending peaks and drops.
8. Key Insights
From the analysis and visualizations, the following insights were derived:
Some expense categories contribute significantly more to overall spending compared to others. Typically, categories such as Rent, Groceries, and Food tend to have high contribution.
Monthly expenses show variation, indicating that spending is not always constant and may depend on seasonal factors, festivals, or personal needs.
Users with higher income do not always have higher savings. Savings depend heavily on how well expenses are controlled.
Categories such as entertainment and shopping may show irregular spending patterns compared to fixed categories like rent and bills.
The monthly trend analysis helps identify months where spending is unusually high, which can be used for planning future budgets.
9. Conclusion
This project successfully analyzed personal expense data and produced meaningful insights using a complete data analysis pipeline. The dataset was cleaned, explored, and analyzed, and visualizations were generated to clearly represent spending behavior.
The results of this analysis can help individuals understand where their money is going, identify unnecessary expenses, and improve monthly savings by making better financial decisions.
